      "title": "11.6.2 Preparation of alkenes",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "b07b1f6c-f5a4-509d-ae86-73be5bffb0d4",
          "pdf_from": 255,
          "pdf_to": 256,
          "note": "Topic 11.6.2 (Preparation of Alkenes), printed pages 255-256."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Alkenes are prepared by the removal of small atoms (H,OH,X) from the adjacent carbon atoms of the saturated compounds, so as to create a double bond between carbon atoms. These reactions are known as elimination reactions. In such reactions, a small molecule such as H₂O or HCl, is removed from adjacent carbons in an organic molecule. Preparation of ethene through various methods is being discussed below."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "i) Dehydration of Alcohols"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Ethene is prepared by heating a mixture of ethanol and excess of concentrated sulphuric acid at 180°C. The reaction involves the removal of a water molecule from the alcohol molecule."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{Ethanol}}{\\ce{CH3CH2OH}} \\ce{->[H2SO4][\\text{heat}]} \\underset{\\text{Ethene}}{\\ce{CH2=CH2}} + \\underset{\\text{water}}{\\ce{H2O}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃CH₂OH (ethanol) → [H₂SO₄, heat] CH₂=CH₂ (ethene) + H₂O (water)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "In the first step, ethyl hydrogen sulphate is formed which decomposes on heating to produce ethene."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{CH3-CH2OH + H2SO4 ->[\\text{180°C}] CH3-CH2-OSO3H + H2O}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–CH₂OH + H₂SO₄ → [180 °C] CH₃–CH₂–OSO₃H + H₂O"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{CH3-CH2OSO3H ->[\\text{heat}] H2C=CH2 + H2SO4}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–CH₂OSO₃H → [heat] H₂C=CH₂ + H₂SO₄"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "ii) Dehydrohalogenation of Alkyl Halides"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "On heating, ethyl bromide with alcoholic KOH, ethene is formed. Removal of hydrogen and halogen takes place from adjacent carbon atoms to create a double bond."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{H3C-CH2-Br + KOH_{(alcoholic)} ->[\\text{heat}] H2C=CH2 + KBr + H2O}",
            "text_alt": "H₃C–CH₂–Br + KOH(alcoholic) → [heat] H₂C=CH₂ + KBr + H₂O"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
